Runs the final induction of a repetitive block with bz2-ng's register-
cursor scans (lib/sort_recursion.h) instead of libsais's, up to a block
size that bz2-ng sets per architecture. Applies after
0005-recursion-prefetch.patch.

libsais_main_8u keeps 0003's decision (bzng_rec_repetitive) in a local.
When it was taken, the block is no longer than BZNG_REC_FINAL_MAX
(lib/sort_libsais.c: 2^17 bytes on x86-64, any length elsewhere) and the
BWT_ROW flag is set, it calls bzng_rec_final_bwt in place of
libsais_induce_final_order_8u_omp, with the same block, entries, bucket
arrays, suffix and row pointer. That function is sais-lite's computeBWT
on the block's bytes, with libsais's bucket arrays and the row of suffix
q noted as 0002 notes it; each scan keeps the current bucket's pointer
in a register, writing it back only when the bucket changes, clears a
marked entry with a select rather than a branch, and does not prefetch.
On a repetitive block consecutive steps mostly write to one bucket, and
libsais's load and store of that bucket's counter at every step form a
chain. It leaves every row's entry, the row of suffix 0, the row of
suffix q and the bucket counters as libsais's final induction leaves
them. It reads the block and writes only the entries and the bucket
counters, so the workspace is unchanged and nothing is allocated. Like
0003, the patched file calls bz2-ng's code, which sort_libsais.c defines
before it; this patch adds only the call.

Measured on the M4 Max, bzng_bwt alone on every block of the MC:Z
corpus, 5 rounds in rotating order against an identical build as
control: blocks of 16 KiB, 64 KiB, 640 kB and whole-file streams sort
1.10-1.11 times as fast with Apple clang 21 and 1.07-1.08 with gcc 16.
Forced onto every block it is slower elsewhere (XA:Z whole-file blocks
0.93 with clang, 0.92 with gcc), which is why only repetitive blocks
take it.

End to end at level 9 against an identical-binary control, 5 rounds,
MC:Z 16 KiB / 64 KiB / whole, with the register path at every size (in a
form of the scans that encoded the entries as libsais does and that
sorts as fast as this one on the M4 with both compilers): Graviton4
1.036 / 1.033 / 1.032 with clang 18 and 1.029 / 1.026 / 1.018 with gcc
13; Zen 5 1.011 / 1.023 / 1.018 with clang 18 and 1.009 / 1.026 / 1.012
with gcc 13; Granite Rapids 1.024 / 1.030 / 0.993 with clang 18 and
1.014 / 1.012 / 0.970 (slower in every round) with gcc 13. So on x86-64
the register path is kept to blocks of up to 2^17 bytes, which covers
every block of a 64 KiB stream (RLE1 lengthens a block by at most a
quarter); between 64 KiB streams and full 900 kB blocks there is no
x86-64 measurement, and above the limit libsais's final induction runs
as before. This form with that limit, every build with
-falign-functions=64, MC:Z 16 KiB / 64 KiB / whole: Graviton4 1.045 /
1.040 / 1.038 with clang 18 and 1.018 / 1.016 / even with gcc 13; Zen 5
1.029 / 1.023 / even with clang 18 and 1.021 / 1.015 / even with gcc 13;
Granite Rapids 1.030 / 1.027 / even with clang 18 and even in all three
with gcc 13; every other cell even, none slower. On the M4 Max, MC:Z
16 KiB / 64 KiB / 640 kB / whole:
1.09-1.11 / 1.09-1.10 / 1.10-1.11 / 1.10 with Apple clang 21 (three
screens) and 1.07 / 1.07 / 1.06-1.07 / 1.06-1.07 with gcc 16 (two),
faster in every round but one (gcc, 16 KiB); XA:Z whole even. These are
short screens of 5 rounds in one hold each against an identical-binary
control, and in each an indexing daemon went over machine-lock's limit,
so they give direction only; clean 5-round screens of the form that
encoded the entries as libsais does gave 1.10 / 1.10 / 1.11 / 1.10 with
clang and 1.07 / 1.06 / 1.07 / 1.06 with gcc, and this form was within
0.015 of that one in every short screen.

--- a/libsais.c
+++ b/libsais.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
  * 0003-repetitive-recursion.patch
  * 0004-keep-lms-bitmap.patch
  * 0005-recursion-prefetch.patch
+ * 0006-repetitive-final-induction.patch
  */
 
 /*--
@@ -6988,6 +6989,7 @@
 
     sa_sint_t m = libsais_count_and_gather_lms_suffixes_8u_omp(T, SA, n, buckets, threads, thread_state);
     sa_sint_t k = libsais_initialize_buckets_start_and_end_8u(buckets, freq);
+    sa_sint_t repetitive = 0;
 
     if ((flags & LIBSAIS_FLAGS_GSA) && (buckets[0] != 0 || buckets[2] != 0 || buckets[3] != 1))
     {
@@ -7037,7 +7039,8 @@
              * (lib/sort_recursion.h, included before this file) in place
              * of libsais's own, with the same arguments and result.
              */
-            sa_sint_t rc = bzng_rec_repetitive(SA + n + fs - m, m, names)
+            repetitive = bzng_rec_repetitive(SA + n + fs - m, m, names);
+            sa_sint_t rc = repetitive
                 ? bzng_rec_sort_main(SA + n + fs - m, SA, fs + n - 2 * m, m, names)
                 : libsais_main_32s_entry(SA + n + fs - m, SA, m, names, fs + n - 2 * m, threads, thread_state);
             if (rc != 0)
@@ -7066,6 +7069,17 @@
     else
     {
         memset(SA, 0, (size_t)n * sizeof(sa_sint_t));
+    }
+
+    /*
+     * The final induction of a repetitive block in BWT mode with the row,
+     * up to bz2-ng's size limit, is bz2-ng's (lib/sort_recursion.h and
+     * lib/sort_libsais.c, included before this file), with the same
+     * arguments and result as the one below.
+     */
+    if (repetitive && n <= BZNG_REC_FINAL_MAX && (flags & LIBSAIS_FLAGS_BWT_ROW))
+    {
+        return bzng_rec_final_bwt(T, SA, n, r, I, &buckets[6 * ALPHABET_SIZE], &buckets[7 * ALPHABET_SIZE]);
     }
 
     return libsais_induce_final_order_8u_omp(T, SA, n, k, flags, r, I, buckets, threads, thread_state);
